Zero-upper-bit integer selection — 2026-10-19

A descending worklist tracks upper zero bits of integer SSA values. A join
uses every incoming edge; entry values remain unknown. Copies, constants,
comparisons, masks and extensions supply bounds. An extension or mask that
cannot change the value becomes a copy of the same destination and class.
W-register copies remain explicit: their high-bit clearing is observable.
No guard, memory access, effect, CFG edge or EvoIR fact changes. This is
ordinary machine selection, not a new certified EqSat rewrite.

Unchanged sources and kernel argv, 100000 calls per case:
Symbol           Before/call After/call GCC14/call Before bytes After bytes
theta                    171        171         94          728         728
pte_offset_map            55         52         23          288         276
folio_state               32         32          9          152         152
Three container and three TCG executions agree on the retained checksums;
no dropped or irregular blocks occur. GCC counts are retained, not rerun.
The final gate-built compiler reproduces all three measured assembly files
byte for byte. The record remains above its 46-instruction exit threshold.
Library-only executed-path check refusals remain non-PASS.

Tests cover joins, loops, signed boundaries, widths 1..63, both targets and
W/X transport. The first full gate exposed stale code-shape expectations and
container sandbox failures. Its failed log remains. All 46 ABI cases were
compared against the old selection: EvoIR is unchanged, and only two target
variants replace a Boolean mask with a W copy. The adjusted snapshots retain
that exact change; no acceptance threshold is weakened.

Final acceptance: 457 Rust suites, 3467 passed, zero failed, eight ignored,
Clippy and formatting pass. All six Lean steps pass: 88434 vectors, 62 rules,
36 analysis rows, 576 enumerated instances and 11 named expectations. The
975 whole-environment axioms are not a TCB count. The separate six-group
Rust/Lean correspondence campaign was not rerun. The shared tree is dirty.

